<urn:atm:triage:finding/AI33-WINDOWS-ADMISSION-BACKPRESSURE-SHUTDOWN-STARVE>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AI33-WINDOWS-ADMISSION-BACKPRESSURE-SHUTDOWN-STARVE" ;
  triage:title "Windows admission-capacity backpressure wait can starve forced shutdown and a full dispatch-handle table crashes the accept loop instead of degrading gracefully" ;
  triage:description "In crates/atm-daemon/src/local_tcp_transport.rs (feature/pAI-s33-admission-capacity-smoke @ 7a1247e4), the new Windows-only accept loop calls wait_for_connection_capacity(&registry) (line ~267) before listener.accept(), which loops on registry.wait_for_connection_change(ACCEPT_POLL_INTERVAL) while active_connections() >= MAX_CONCURRENT_CONNECTIONS (64). This wait is NOT interrupted by lifecycle.terminate_requested() -- that flag is only checked at the top of the outer loop, before this call -- so if a handler thread wedges and never releases its ActiveConnectionGuard, the accept loop is trapped inside wait_for_connection_capacity indefinitely and never reaches the shutdown branch, defeating drain_active_connections_for_shutdown's own force_cancel_deadline guarantee. Separately, spawn_connection's fallback path when registry.push_dispatch_handle's own bounded dispatch-handle-table capacity check fails (active_connection_registry.rs push_dispatch_handle, handles.len() >= max_handles) is NOT routed through write_saturated_response like the try_register rejection path is -- instead push_dispatch_handle synchronously calls handle.join_handle.join() on the accept-loop thread itself (blocking up to REQUEST_DEADLINE=3s), then returns Err, which propagates all the way out of serve_with_runtime_hooks via spawn_connection(...)? and terminates the entire daemon accept/serve loop. This dispatch-handle-table saturation path is reachable because dispatch_handles length is only trimmed opportunistically via reap_finished_dispatches, so it can lag active_connections and hit max_handles even while try_register still had headroom. Both issues undermine this sprint's own stated goal of graceful admission handling under Windows local-TCP capacity pressure. Suggested fix: (1) make wait_for_connection_capacity re-check terminate_requested() (or use a select/notify pattern that shutdown can also signal) so shutdown is never blocked behind capacity backpressure; (2) route the push_dispatch_handle rejection path through write_saturated_response (or equivalent graceful 503) instead of propagating a fatal Err, and avoid a synchronous join() on the accept-loop thread." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-AI" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "correctness" ;
  triage:severity "blocking" ;
